= Solution
{parent=i}

Writing $c_i(t)=\langle\mathbf1_{[0,t]},f_i\rangle_{L^2}$ gives $X_n(t)=\sum_{i=1}^n\alpha_ic_i(t)$, hence
$$
X_n(t)\sim N\left(0,\sum_{i=1}^nc_i(t)^2\right).
$$
By <Parseval identity>, the variance tends to $\lVert\mathbf1_{[0,t]}\rVert_2^2=t$. Thus $X_n(t)\xrightarrow dN(0,t)$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For fixed $t$, $(X_n(t))$ is a martingale with independent centered increments and
$$
\sup_n\mathbb E X_n(t)^2=\sum_{i\geq1}c_i(t)^2=t.
$$
The $L^2$ <martingale convergence theorem> gives convergence both almost surely and in $L^2$ to a random variable $X(t)$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For real $u_1,\ldots,u_j$, the preceding series construction gives
$$
\sum_ru_rX(t_r)
=\sum_{i\geq1}\alpha_i\left\langle\sum_ru_r\mathbf1_{[0,t_r]},f_i\right\rangle.
$$
It is centered Gaussian, and <Parseval identity> makes its variance
$$
\left\lVert\sum_ru_r\mathbf1_{[0,t_r]}\right\rVert_2^2
=\sum_{r,s}u_ru_s\min(t_r,t_s).
$$
This is the variance of $\sum_ru_rB_{t_r}$. The <Cramer-Wold theorem> proves equality of the two joint distributions.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

