= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $Z$ be a fair Bernoulli variable measurable at time zero and let $(S_n)$ be an independent simple symmetric random walk. Then $X_n=ZS_n$ is a martingale with increments bounded by one. On $\{Z=0\}$ it converges to zero, while on $\{Z=1\}$ the recurrence of the <simple symmetric random walk> gives limsup $+\infty$ and liminf $-\infty$. Thus $\mathbb P(A)=\mathbb P(B)=1/2$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Set $p_n=\mathbb P(B_n\mid\mathcal F_{n-1})$, $S_n=\sum_{j\leq n}\mathbf1_{B_j}$, and $A_n=\sum_{j\leq n}p_j$. Then $M_n=S_n-A_n$ is a martingale with bounded increments and conditional variance at most $p_n$. On $\{A_\infty<\infty\}$, localization and the $L^2$ martingale convergence theorem make $M_n$ converge, so the integer-valued increasing sequence $S_n$ is finite. On $\{A_\infty=\infty\}$, applying martingale convergence to
$$
\sum_n\frac{\mathbf1_{B_n}-p_n}{1+A_n}
$$
and <Kronecker lemma> gives $M_n/A_n\to0$. Hence $S_n/A_n\to1$ and $S_n\to\infty$. This is the <conditional Borel-Cantelli lemma>, and proves the two events equal almost surely.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

